 17592186045417] [17592186434747 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186434680 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434747 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186434684 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434747 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186434688 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434747 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186434689 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434747 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186434716 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434747 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434747 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434747 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186434684 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434747 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "caaf0180-b775-51a5-8357-5dbad212c145" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434748 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186434656 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434748 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186434687 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434748 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186434708 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434748 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186434729 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434748 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186434733 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434748 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434748 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434748 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186434656 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434748 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d9aa5f48-03a4-5e22-bfab-3a430f54be65" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434757 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186475173 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186434757 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186475174 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186434757 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186475175 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186434757 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186475176 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186434757 #edb/ref 1069 "q-164168" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434757 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434757 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/f3/f3852805b0ae44433828113396fa63d0688ec06ae57886428815515e521baa49.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the points shown are local minimum points?\n\nSelect all that apply." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186434757 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7f6e52f3-8502-516e-a6ab-c65771746796" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434757 #edb/ref 1074 "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum point at $(a,b)$ if $f(x,y)\\ge f(a,b)$ for all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b).$ \n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the lowest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nSuch regions exist for the points $B$ and $D.$ Hence, $B$ and $D$ are local minimum points.\n\n**Note**: For the point $D({x}_{D},{y}_{D},{z}_{D}),$ there are points $(x,y)$ in the surrounding regions such that $f({x}_{D},{y}_{D})=f(x,y).$ However, since the definition of local extrema is non-strict, $f(x,y)\\ge f(a,b)$, the point $D$ does satisfy the conditions for a local minimum.\n\n![](images/f1/f10742ca92d2429c35b36cf77ae2d857774faba848a74291bbfa8e5ac71eeada.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434760 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186434748 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434760 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163774" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434760 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434760 #edb/ref 1071 "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈2+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -6\\end{bmatrix}(x-a).\n$$\n\nGiven that the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1+\\sqrt{26}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1-\\sqrt{26},$ what type of point is $a?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186434760 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ae219904-d456-5249-bf4b-05599f3e75b5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434760 #edb/ref 1074 "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nThe quadratic Taylor expansion of a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{n}$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSince the quadratic Taylor expansion of $f$ has no $(x-a)$ term, we can conclude that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ Hence, $a$ is a critical point of $f.$\n\nFinally, notice that ${f}^{″}(a)$ has both negative and positive eigenvalues:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & =-1+\\sqrt{26}>0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{2} & =-1-\\sqrt{26}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite. Therefore, $a$ is a saddle point of $f.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434763 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186475165 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186434763 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186475166 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186434763 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186475167 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186434763 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186475168 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186434763 #edb/ref 1069 "q-164101" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434763 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434763 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/f4/f4ca398cfb365346962fc66ffecd7fb85390a9a98c49cb1926f6d8093a01c8bc.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the points shown are local minimum points?\n\nSelect all that apply." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186434763 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e433d738-c2a7-5a16-852b-741bb762e1fe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434763 #edb/ref 1074 "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum point at $(a,b)$ if $f(x,y)\\ge f(a,b)$ for all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b).$ \n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x